 },
 "paragraphs": [
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p001.s01",
     "source": "###"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p001"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p002.s01",
     "source": "Maurice Connor was the king, and that's no small word, of all the pipers in Munster."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s02",
     "source": "He could play jig and planxty without end, and Ollistrum's March, and the Eagle's Whistle, and the Hen's Concert, and odd tunes of every sort and kind."
    },
    {
     "id": "p002.s03",
     "source": "But he knew one, far more surprising than the rest, which had in it the power to set every thing dead or alive dancing."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p002"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p003.s01",
     "source": "In what way he learned it is beyond my knowledge, for he was mighty cautious about telling how he came by so wonderful a tune."
    },
    {
     "id": "p003.s02",
     "source": "At the very first note of that tune, the brogues began shaking upon the feet of all who heard it—old or young it mattered not—just as if their brogues had the ague; then the feet began going—going—going from under them, and at last up and away with them, dancing like mad!—whisking here, there, and every where, like a straw in a storm—there was no halting while the music lasted!"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p003"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p004.s01",
     "source": "Not a fair, nor a wedding, nor a patron in the seven parishes round, was counted worth the speaking of without \"blind Maurice and his pipes.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "p004.s02",
     "source": "His mother, poor woman, used to lead him about from one place to another, just like a dog."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p004"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p005.s01",
     "source": "Down through Iveragh—a place that ought to be proud of itself, for 'tis Daniel O'Connell's country—Maurice Connor and his mother were taking their rounds."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s02",
     "source": "Beyond all other places Iveragh is the place for stormy coast and steep mountains: as proper a spot it is as any in Ireland to get yourself drowned, or your neck broken on the land, should you prefer that."
    },
    {
     "id": "p005.s03",
     "source": "But, notwithstanding, in Ballinskellig bay there is a neat bit of ground, well fitted for diversion, and down from it towards the water, is a clean smooth piece of strand—the dead image of a calm summer's sea on a moonlight night, with just the curl of the small waves upon it."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p005"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p006.s01",
     "source": "Here it was that Maurice's music had brought from all parts a great gathering of the young men and the young women— O the darlints! —for 'twas not every day the strand of Trafraska was stirred up by the voice of a bagpipe."
    },
    {
     "id": "p006.s02",
     "source": "The dance began; and as pretty a rinkafadda it was as ever was danced."
    },
    {
     "id": "p006.s03",
     "source": "\"Brave music,\" said every body, \"and well done,\" when Maurice stopped."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p006"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p007.s01",
     "source": "\"More power to your elbow, Maurice, and a fair wind in the bellows,\" cried Paddy Dorman, a hump-backed dancing-master, who was there to keep order. \"'Tis a pity,\" said he, \"if we'd let the piper run dry after such music; 'twould be a disgrace to Iveragh, that didn't come on it since the week of the three Sundays.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "p007.s02",
     "source": "So, as well became him, for he was always a decent man, says he: \"Did you drink, piper?\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p007"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p008.s01",
     "source": "\"I will, sir,\" says Maurice, answering the question on the safe side, for you never yet knew piper or schoolmaster who refused his drink."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p008"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p009.s01",
     "source": "\"What will you drink, Maurice?\" says Paddy."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p009"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p010.s01",
     "source": "\"I'm no ways particular,\" says Maurice; \"I drink any thing, and give God thanks, barring raw water; but if 'tis all the same to you, mister Dorman, may be you wouldn't lend me the loan of a glass of whiskey.\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p010"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p011.s01",
     "source": "\"I've no glass, Maurice,\" said Paddy; \"I've only the bottle.\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p011"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p012.s01",
     "source": "\"Let that be no hindrance,\" answered Maurice; \"my mouth just holds a glass to the drop; often I've tried it, sure.\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p012"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p013.s01",
     "source": "So Paddy Dorman trusted him with the bottle—more fool was he; and, to his cost, he found that though Maurice's mouth might not hold more than the glass at one time, yet, owing to the hole in his throat, it took many a filling."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p013"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p014.s01",
     "source": "\"That was no bad whisky neither,\" says Maurice, handing back the empty bottle."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p014"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p015.s01",
     "source": "\"By the holy frost, then!\" says Paddy, \"'tis but cowld comfort there's in that bottle now; and 'tis your word we must take for the strength of the whisky, for you've left us no sample to judge by:\" and to be sure Maurice had not."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p015"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p016.s01",
     "source": "Now I need not tell any gentleman or lady with common understanding, that if he or she was to drink an honest bottle of whiskey at one pull, it is not at all the same thing as drinking a bottle of water; and in the whole course of my life, I never knew more than five men who could do so without being overtaken by the liquor."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s02",
     "source": "Of these Maurice Connor was not one, though he had a stiff head enough of his own—he was fairly tipsy."
    },
    {
     "id": "p016.s03",
     "source": "Don't think I blame him for it; 'tis often a good man's case; but true is the word that says, \"when liquor's in, sense is out;\" and puff, at a breath, before you could say \"Lord save us!\" out he blasted his wonderful tune."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p016"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p017.s01",
     "source": "'Twas really then beyond all belief or telling the dancing Maurice himself could not keep quiet; staggering now on one leg, now on the other, and rolling about like a ship in a cross sea, trying to humour the tune."
    },
    {
     "id": "p017.s02",
     "source": "There was his mother too, moving her old bones as light as the youngest girl of them all; but her dancing, no, nor the dancing of all the rest, is not worthy the speaking about to the work that was going on down on the strand."
    },
    {
     "id": "p017.s03",
     "source": "Every inch of it covered with all manner of fish jumping and plunging about to the music, and every moment more and more would tumble in out of the water, charmed by the wonderful tune."
    },
    {
     "id": "p017.s04",
     "source": "Crabs of monstrous size spun round and round on one claw with the nimbleness of a dancing-master, and twirled and tossed their other claws about like limbs that did not belong to them."
    },
    {
     "id": "p017.s05",
     "source": "It was a sight surprising to behold."
    },
    {
     "id": "p017.s06",
     "source": "But perhaps you may have heard of father Florence Conry, a Franciscan Friar, and a great Irish poet; bolg an dana , as they used to call him—a wallet of poems."
    },
    {
     "id": "p017.s07",
     "source": "If you have not he was as pleasant a man as one would wish to drink with of a hot summer's day; and he has rhymed out all about the dancing fishes so neatly, that it would be a thousand pities not to give you his verses; so here's my hand at an upset of them into English:"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p017"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p018.s01",
     "source": "Never was such an ullabulloo in this world, before or since; 'twas as if heaven and earth were coming together; and all out of Maurice Connor's wonderful tune!"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p018"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p019.s01",
     "source": "In the height of all these doings, what should there be dancing among the outlandish set of fishes but a beautiful young woman—as beautiful as the dawn of day!"
    },
    {
     "id": "p019.s02",
     "source": "She had a cocked hat upon her head; from under it her long green hair—just the colour of the sea—fell down behind, without hinderance to her dancing."
    },
    {
     "id": "p019.s03",
     "source": "Her teeth were like rows of pearl; her lips for all the world looked like red coral; and she had an elegant gown, as white as the foam of the wave, with little rows of purple and red sea-weeds settled out upon it; for you never yet saw a lady, under the water or over the water, who had not a good notion of dressing herself out."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p019"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p020.s01",
     "source": "Up she danced at last to Maurice, who was flinging his feet from under him as fast as hops—for nothing in this world could keep still while that tune of his was going on—and says she to him, chaunting it out with a voice as sweet as honey—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p020"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p021.s01",
     "source": "Drink was strong in Maurice's head, and out he chaunted in return for her great civility."
    },
    {
     "id": "p021.s02",
     "source": "It is not every lady, may be, that would be after making such an offer to a blind piper; therefore 'twas only right in him to give her as good as she gave herself—so says Maurice,"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p021"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p022.s01",
     "source": "The lady looked at him quite amazed, and swinging her head from side to side like a great scholar, \"Well,\" says she, \"Maurice, if you're not a poet, where is poetry to be found?\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p022"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p023.s01",
     "source": "In this way they kept on at it, framing high compliments; one answering the other, and their feet going with the music as fast as their tongues."
    },
    {
     "id": "p023.s02",
     "source": "All the fish kept dancing too: Maurice heard the clatter, and was afraid to stop playing lest it might be displeasing to the fish, and not knowing what so many of them may take it into their heads to do to him if they got vexed."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p023"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p024.s01",
     "source": "Well, the lady with the green hair kept on coaxing of Maurice with soft speeches, till at last she overpersuaded him to promise to marry her, and be king over the fishes, great and small."
    },
    {
     "id": "p024.s02",
     "source": "Maurice was well fitted to be their king, if they wanted one that could make them dance; and he surely would drink, barring the salt water, with any fish of them all."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p024"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p025.s01",
     "source": "When Maurice's mother saw him, with that unnatural thing in the form of a green-haired lady as his guide, and he and she dancing down together so lovingly to the water's edge through the thick of the fishes, she called out after him to stop and come back."
    },
    {
     "id": "p025.s02",
     "source": "\"Oh then,\" says she, \"as if I was not widow enough before, there he is going away from me to be married to that scaly woman."
    },
    {
     "id": "p025.s03",
     "source": "And who knows but 'tis grandmother I may be to a hake or a cod—Lord help and pity me, but 'tis a mighty unnatural thing!—and may be 'tis boiling and eating my own grandchild I'll be, with a bit of salt butter, and I not knowing it!—Oh Maurice, Maurice, if there's any love or nature left in you, come back to your own ould mother, who reared you like a decent Christian!\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p025"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p026.s01",
     "source": "Then the poor woman began to cry and ullagoane so finely that it would do any one good to hear her."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p026"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p027.s01",
     "source": "Maurice was not long getting to the rim of the water; there he kept playing and dancing on as if nothing was the matter, and a great thundering wave coming in towards him ready to swallow him up alive; but as he could not see it, he did not fear it."
    },
    {
     "id": "p027.s02",
     "source": "His mother it was who saw it plainly through the big tears that were rolling down her cheeks; and though she saw it, and her heart was aching as much as ever mother's heart ached for a son, she kept dancing, dancing, all the time for the bare life of her."
    },
    {
     "id": "p027.s03",
     "source": "Certain it was she could not help it, for Maurice never stopped playing that wonderful tune of his."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p027"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p028.s01",
     "source": "He only turned the bothered ear to the sound of his mother's voice, fearing it might put him out in his steps, and all the answer he made back was—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p028"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p029.s01",
     "source": "\"Whisht with you, mother—sure I'm going to be king over the fishes down in the sea, and for a token of luck, and a sign that I am alive and well, I'll send you in, every twelvemonth on this day, a piece of burned wood to Trafraska.\""
    },
    {
     "id": "p029.s02",
     "source": "Maurice had not the power to say a word more, for the strange lady with the green hair, seeing the wave just upon them, covered him up with herself in a thing like a cloak with a big hood to it, and the wave curling over twice as high as their heads, burst upon the strand, with a rush and a roar that might be heard as far as Cape Clear."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p029"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p030.s01",
     "source": "That day twelvemonth the piece of burned wood came ashore in Trafraska."
    },
    {
     "id": "p030.s02",
     "source": "It was a queer thing for Maurice to think of sending all the way from the bottom of the sea."
    },
    {
     "id": "p030.s03",
     "source": "A gown or a pair of shoes would have been something like a present for his poor mother; but he had said it, and he kept his word."
    },
    {
     "id": "p030.s04",
     "source": "The bit of burned wood regularly came ashore on the appointed day for as good, ay, and better than a hundred years."
    },
    {
     "id": "p030.s05",
     "source": "The day is now forgotten, and may be that is the reason why people say how Maurice Connor has stopped sending the luck-token to his mother."
    },
    {
     "id": "p030.s06",
     "source": "Poor woman, she did not live to get as much as one of them; for what through the loss of Maurice, and the fear of eating her own grandchildren, she died in three weeks after the dance—some say it was the fatigue that killed her, but whichever it was, Mrs. Connor was decently buried with her own people."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p030"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p031.s01",
     "source": "Seafaring men have often heard off the coast of Kerry, on a still night, the sound of music coming up from the water; and some, who have had good ears, could plainly distinguish Maurice Connor's voice singing these words to his pipes:—"
    }
   ],
   "id": "p031"
  },
  {
